  card="Sunlight passed through a narrow slit and a glass prism gives a band of colours: violet, indigo, blue, green, yellow, orange and red (VIBGYOR). The band of coloured components of a light beam is called its spectrum, and the splitting of light into its component colours is called dispersion. Different colours of light bend through different angles with respect to the incident ray as they pass through a prism: red bends the least and violet the most. So rays of each colour emerge along different paths and become distinct. Isaac Newton was the first to use a glass prism to obtain the spectrum of sunlight. He could not split the colours further with a second prism, but a second identical prism in an inverted position recombined the colours into white light, so sunlight is made up of seven colours.",
